  nodeName: The Insulin Crystal Dissolves
  nodePreview: {fileID: 0}
  nodeNumber: 0
  nodeNexus: 9
  nodeType: 1
  nodeConstituents: 18000000
  nodePositions: []
  nodeAbstract: 
  nodeTextLayers:
  - title: What is happening to the insulin crystal?
    basic: "Once the vesicle and cell membranes fuse, a channel opens up, letting
      water into the vesicle environment. \nThe insulin in the vesicle environment
      becomes less concentrated and the insulin crystal starts dissolving. "
    intermediate: 
    advanced: "The exocytosis exposes the insulin crystal to interstitial fluid which
      has low insulin concentration, thus causing the crystal to dissolve. \nThe
      hydrophilic nature of insulin hexamers combined with the neutral pH and water
      in the interstitial fluid help facilitate the dissolving process."
  - title: Why is it important that the insulin crystal is dissolving?
    basic: "Insulin regulates processes all over the body. \nDissolved insulin can
      traverse quickly through the bloodstream to reach target organs. \nInsulin
      crystals densely house many insulin molecules. \nReleasing insulin from a dense
      deposit supplies insulin to the whole body quickly.\r"
    intermediate: 
    advanced: 
  nodeSpriteLayer: []
